    "origin_url": "https://www.powermag.com/grid-resilience-is-about-more-than-adding-capacity/",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-02T22:32:37.464178+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.powermag.com/grid-resilience-is-about-more-than-adding-capacity/",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.powermag.com/grid-resilience-is-about-more-than-adding-capacity/",
    "origin_publisher": "POWER Magazine",
    "published_at": "2026-11-02T21:12:31+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-02T21:12:31+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-02T22:37:15.907216+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-02T22:37:15.907216+00:00",
    "body": "How much more generation will the U.S. need to meet rising electricity demand? It’s an important question, but it isn’t the only one the power industry needs to answer. As data centers, advanced manufacturing, and broader electrification increase demand, the growing challenge is delivering power where and when it is needed.\nCOMMENTARY\nNew generation can add capacity to the system, but it doesn’t necessarily resolve transmission constraints, interconnection delays, or periods when local demand outpaces available supply. That puts a premium on flexibility.\nEnergy storage can provide flexibility at multiple points across the power system, from utility-scale installations to resources deployed behind the meter at data centers and other mission-critical facilities. Realizing its full value will require the industry to move beyond thinking about storage as a single asset class. Different grid challenges require different response times, discharge durations, and operating characteristics, and the storage strategy should reflect those differences.\nWhy Grid Resilience Requires More Flexibility\nThe power industry is simultaneously managing rapid load growth and infrastructure that can take years to expand. That mismatch matters. New generation projects do little to relieve a local constraint if the transmission infrastructure needed to move that power is not available. Likewise, adding generating capacity does not necessarily address short periods of extreme demand or provide the rapid response needed when conditions change unexpectedly. Storage creates another option.\nEnergy storage can help address gaps between when and where power is available and when it is needed. That can include utility-scale storage as well as systems deployed closer to the load.\nThe latter will become increasingly important as data centers, manufacturing plants, hospitals, and other large energy users invest in more sophisticated on-site power systems. Those resources will primarily serve the reliability needs of the facilities themselves, but where capacity is available, they can also push power back out to the grid to provide another source of flexibility during high-stress periods.\nEnergy storage isn’t a substitute for investment in generation or transmission; those investments remain essential. However, it can help the industry make better use of existing infrastructure while longer-term projects are developed.\nDifferent Energy Storage Applications Require Different Battery Technologies\nAs the role of storage expands, the industry also needs to move past the idea that one battery technology will emerge as the universal solution. Grid resilience is not one application.\nA system providing instantaneous response or short-duration ride-through is performing a fundamentally different job than a system shifting several hours of renewable generation. A facility maintaining critical operations through a prolonged outage has another set of requirements. Those differences matter when selecting battery technology.\nFor shorter-duration applications requiring reliable high-power performance, established battery technologies such as advanced lead and lithium can play important roles. As discharge requirements extend, technologies designed for long-duration energy storage, including vanadium redox flow batteries, become increasingly beneficial.\nIn some cases, the most resilient solution may incorporate more than one battery technology. A hybrid system could use one battery technology for rapid response and another for longer-duration energy needs. Rather than forcing a single chemistry to satisfy every requirement, this technology-agnostic approach allows the storage architecture to be designed around the actual operating profile.\nThe question should not be which battery chemistry wins. It should be which combination of technologies best addresses the specific reliability and resilience requirements of the application.\nWhy Domestic Manufacturing and Recycling Matter\nA battery system may remain in service for decades. During that time, operators need confidence that replacement components, technical expertise, and service support will remain available. A strong supply chain is therefore essential to the technology’s long-term resilience.\nRecent disruptions have demonstrated the risk of depending heavily on globally concentrated supply chains for critical materials and components. For energy storage systems that may ultimately support critical grid and facility operations, domestic manufacturing capacity, access to materials, and established recycling infrastructure can reduce another source of uncertainty.\nRecycling is particularly important because it can create a domestic source of materials for future battery production rather than leaving the industry entirely dependent on newly mined or imported resources.\nThe lead battery industry provides one established example of that model, with a mature domestic recycling network that recovers materials from end-of-life batteries and returns them to manufacturing. As newer battery technologies scale, developing similarly robust circular supply chains should be part of the industry’s long-term resilience strategy.\nBuilding Resilience Around More Options\nThe grid challenges emerging today will not be solved by a single technology. The U.S. will need additional generation, expanded transmission and distribution infrastructure, and continued grid modernization. Large energy users will also need to take a more active role in managing their own power requirements. Energy storage can help connect those efforts by giving the system more options when conditions change.\nA resilient grid is ultimately one that can adapt. Building more flexibility into the system across technologies, durations, and locations will give grid operators and energy users more ways to respond.\n— Scott Childers is vice president, Industry & Technology, with Stryten Energy .\nTagged in:\nbattery recycling\ngrid flexibility\ngrid resilience\nhybrid storage\nlead batteries\nlithium batteries\nlong-duration storage\nStryten Energy\nvanadium flow\nSept. 28—30, 2026\nWashington, D.C.\nRegister Now\nExplore this topic and more — live at Experience POWER. The power industry's most urgent decisions are being made right now. Be in the room where they happen.\nView Conference Program",
